{"connection_id": "BC-bd3e4669bf33ad480f00", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tested faith produces endurance", "note": "James says the testing of faith produces endurance; this gives the commentary's ṣabr a process rooted in belief rather than mere physical toughness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac89bb9cb2987ea000d9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "testing produces endurance", "note": "The commentary makes patience the high ground of a hard route; James says tested faith produces endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5706b01dbb69888d81b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance completes its work", "note": "James says endurance must complete its work so the hearer becomes mature and whole; this joins patience, action, and a result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f8a483d728228af3afd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance completes its work", "note": "The commentary joins work with the patience needed to reach the top; James urges endurance to finish its work so people may become complete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2def0763bff60b3cf893", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith without works is dead", "note": "James says faith without works is dead; this closely matches the commentary's pairing of belief with righteous deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cafbdad2e121a79d449d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith without works is dead", "note": "The commentary's trusted mount and working beast join faith to conduct; James says faith without works has no life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2885c2be1c91da782ad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith completed by works", "note": "James says Abraham's faith was active together with his works and completed by them; belief and action are mutually expressed rather than separated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bf20875dd1564c1a33c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith active with works", "note": "The commentary places trustworthiness and work together on the journey; James says Abraham's faith worked with his actions and reached completion through them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13d5f747f36699b0481e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.24", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "justified by works and not faith alone", "note": "James explicitly says a person is justified by works and not by faith alone; beside the surah's joined faith-and-deed formula, this shows a Christian argument over how the two relate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd7925f45d9e202473c0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way and truth", "note": "Jesus calls himself the way and the truth; this is a specific meeting of the commentary's road image with its developed ḥaqq/truth image, though John's claim is about access to the Father."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a47f421f9f9f8b16a859", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus bears cross to execution place", "note": "The commentary's human carries a consequential burden to the journey's end; John depicts Jesus carrying his cross to the Place of a Skull."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd8e87f62dee9aa12485", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.4.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus tired from his journey", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a reliable animal from a human traveler; Jesus himself is wearied by his journey and sits beside a well."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff2607182efabef1825e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "traveler on the road", "note": "A man traveling down from Jerusalem to Jericho is attacked and left half dead; this supplies a concrete road scene in which a human traveler becomes vulnerable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ebb53832d148f9a39d9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.34", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wounded man carried on a beast", "note": "The Samaritan binds the man's wounds and puts him on his own animal; the beast carries a human who cannot continue under his own strength."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37012d6db81b1f83877b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.34", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "injured traveler placed on own beast", "note": "The commentary calls care for the mount good action; the Samaritan uses his own animal to carry a wounded road traveler and takes him for care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbeaa5553d16d9677d98", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.35", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "continued care", "note": "The Samaritan leaves money and instructs the innkeeper to care for the wounded traveler; the aid begun on the road continues as practical responsibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef3cc6a402040bc8f107", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.46", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lawyers impose burdens they will not touch", "note": "The commentary condemns forcing another beyond capacity; Jesus says legal experts load people with hard burdens while refusing to touch the loads themselves."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f134f7b39f6075cdad4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.13.15", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "untie and water the animal", "note": "Jesus says people untie an ox or donkey on the Sabbath to lead it to water; this directly treats animal care as a legitimate obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-397e540b75ba3787e4ed", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.13.15", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "untie donkey or ox for water", "note": "The commentary calls humane treatment of an animal good action; Jesus argues that people lead an ox or donkey to water even on the Sabbath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e9dbd9c903518807ec6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.14.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rescue an ox from a well", "note": "Jesus asks who would not immediately pull a son or ox from a well even on Sabbath; the animal's danger makes rescue more urgent than ritual restriction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27750e451e25cf6bff07", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.14.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ox rescued from well", "note": "The commentary makes right treatment of a beast an ethical act; Jesus appeals to rescuing an ox from a well even on the Sabbath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30fb954bf6df5cc76026", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "man carries found sheep on shoulders", "note": "The commentary depicts the human using an animal to carry a load; the shepherd joyfully carries the recovered animal himself, reversing that relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-063e422adbe20f3632f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.9.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carry the cross daily", "note": "Jesus tells followers to deny themselves, take up their cross daily, and follow him; this makes a carried burden part of a sustained path of discipleship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0438fcb63dd85af239f7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.9.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "daily cross carried while following", "note": "The commentary connects a human burden to travel; Jesus tells followers to take up their cross daily and follow him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b43df5d03e648368a1b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.10.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camel and impossible passage", "note": "Jesus compares a camel passing through a needle's eye with a rich person entering God's kingdom; the image reverses the camel's ordinary capacity to carry loads and travel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1165fa1d72a85c46c27", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.2.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "friends carry a paralytic", "note": "Four people bring a paralytic to Jesus by carrying him; this is a concrete scene of humans bearing another person's weight so he can reach help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8edb0f1557c7dd08f753", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heavy-laden people invited to rest", "note": "Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come for rest; this gives the commentary's human load an answer in relief rather than further exertion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a108e7c51c3ccb8ec32", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weary and heavily burdened", "note": "The commentary sets the limit at what a carrier can endure; Jesus invites the weary and burdened to him for rest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e4cd8b9664544d208d6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoke and rest", "note": "Jesus asks disciples to take his yoke and learn from him, promising rest; the yoke is a chosen teaching relation rather than an overloaded animal's burden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9367dbb243960b0010d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.29", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoke and rest", "note": "The commentary imagines a human traveler under a load; Jesus offers his yoke and learning from him as the way to rest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-438c8d39098469db353d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "easy yoke and light burden", "note": "Jesus calls his yoke easy and burden light, a direct contrast to the commentary's camel driven beyond its strength."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54ee4388eb4f58e3f89e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.11.30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "easy yoke and light burden", "note": "The commentary contrasts a fit burden with exhausting a beast; Jesus calls his own yoke easy and his burden light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b0c54bba9d5743ae4186", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sheep rescued from pit", "note": "The commentary condemns leaving an animal to suffer under strain; Jesus assumes one would lift a sheep from a pit even on the Sabbath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad82bf5c23d791661917", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animal rescue supports doing good", "note": "The commentary makes kindness to an animal a good act; after the sheep-in-a-pit example, Jesus says doing good on the Sabbath is lawful and compares human worth with the sheep's."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24546073ec47f050f4f7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.16.27", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deeds and recompense", "note": "The Son of Man repays each according to what they have done; this supplies a concrete outcome-for-action formula beside the commentary's claim that how the load is carried determines the result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb7fbcfd3ef37650d20a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.21.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "humble king riding donkey", "note": "The commentary's rider and mount convey how a journey is made; Matthew quotes the prophecy of a humble king arriving mounted on a donkey and colt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-548424b3615648de9a8a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.23.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "burdens laid on shoulders", "note": "Jesus says scribes and Pharisees bind heavy burdens on others' shoulders but will not move them with a finger; the image indicts the human driver who imposes loads without helping."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9d3d36fc862ee0f17b8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.23.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "heavy burdens laid on others", "note": "The commentary condemns driving an animal beyond its capacity; Jesus condemns teachers who bind heavy loads on human shoulders yet will not move them with a finger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d8a4aaa78492bd3c2b7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.24.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endure to end and be saved", "note": "The commentary's load has an outcome at journey's end; Jesus says the one enduring to the end will be saved."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f557fffcb66df64edef6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two roads and destruction", "note": "Jesus contrasts the broad way leading to destruction with an entrance many choose; this joins the commentary's road image to a moral outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d89f78232ec30f8b081", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad way to destruction", "note": "The commentary's road leads to gain or loss according to its use; Jesus contrasts the wide way ending in destruction with entry through the narrow gate."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-c24460d03edce5effce2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hard way to life", "note": "The narrow gate and constricted way lead to life and are found by few; this resembles the commentary's steep route and the effort required to reach its outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-86cc5bf64e4299c6dcf3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow way to life", "note": "The commentary's steep moral route has an outcome; Jesus calls the way to life narrow and hard to find."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0d1689e05a7df0e17bf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.16", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "running and laboring not in vain", "note": "The commentary combines the road, human work, and the danger of loss; Paul hopes at Christ's day to find that he neither ran nor labored in vain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8edf011ca56fa42353ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "press toward the goal", "note": "The commentary's human is a traveler rather than merely a carrier; Paul presses toward the goal for the upward call's prize."}
{"connection_id": "BC-460b8f4954d6ecadf042", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance, commandments, and faith", "note": "The verse defines the saints by endurance, keeping God's commandments, and faith in Jesus; it brings together the commentary's patience, good action, and belief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d516d8fab459ab2a89d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "works, faith, service, and endurance", "note": "The message to Thyatira lists works, love, faith, service, and patient endurance, and says the later works exceed the first; this is a close formula joining belief, action, and perseverance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aff1256e97bc14a84ee6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work, toil, and endurance", "note": "The message to Ephesus names its works, toil, and patient endurance; it brings together the commentary's labor and persistence in one concrete community assessment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-515c7737503e5df06801", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.24", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no additional burden imposed", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a fitting load from excessive demands; the speaker says he lays no other burden on the faithful hearers at Thyatira."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16615bbbbb4a9748a50f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hold fast until arrival", "note": "The commentary's bearer needs patience through the whole way; the next verse tells those hearers to hold fast what they have until the speaker comes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f6a3285b7ae270af957", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.2.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance without weariness", "note": "The Ephesians have endured and not grown weary; the wording adds the specific question of fatigue developed in the commentary's reliable, untiring mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76daf9a8a6895d92a8aa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.15.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the strong bear the weak", "note": "Paul says the strong ought to bear the weaknesses of the weak rather than please themselves; this makes carrying a communal duty of care, not only an individual's burden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30e3a0432fa24a1b929e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.15.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strong bear weaknesses of weak", "note": "The commentary's burden image implies attention to what another can carry; Paul asks the strong to bear the weaknesses of those without strength."}
{"connection_id": "BC-844699e862813791c061", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "persistence in doing good", "note": "Paul describes eternal life for those who persist in doing good; this joins the commentary's righteous deeds with its patience and outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d51425ed6744635ceca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patient good work toward life", "note": "The commentary's right work and patience matter at the end of the journey; Paul speaks of eternal life for those persisting in good work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0af5b1decc7545edb646", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "suffering produces endurance", "note": "Paul says affliction produces endurance; it gives the commentary's ṣabr on the stony ascent a specific process rather than a static virtue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-969f559df778f3e40e2f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance yields character and hope", "note": "Paul continues that endurance produces tested character and hope; it extends the commentary's mountain-road patience into a sequence with an outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a110d250dbee329fb6d", "ref": "1QS 3:20-4:26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two spirits and their ways", "note": "The Rule of the Community contrasts the ways and lots of truth and perversity and their final outcomes; it offers a Jewish ethical map of conduct and consequence beside the commentary's human path from loss to right action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6caabbc81fef8ece983c", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:6-9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow path to inheritance", "note": "Ezra describes one narrow, dangerous path between fire and water leading to a city and inheritance; it makes hardship on the way a condition of reaching the destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2807804ddd514bd08c6a", "ref": "Bava Metzia 32a", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "relieving an animal's load", "note": "The rabbinic discussion of unloading and loading reads the biblical commands through the animal's suffering and the duty to relieve it; this sharpens the commentary's limit against overdriving a beast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3cde70f7db58c2b8ff7b", "ref": "Bava Metzia 32b", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "animal suffering and unloading", "note": "The commentary opposes an unbearable load on a beast; rabbinic discussion of unloading the fallen animal treats relief of animal suffering as part of the command's force."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f798274083c3fa78cdb2", "ref": "Bava Metzia 32b", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "first aid even for enemy's beast", "note": "The commentary tests a rider's goodness by the animal's welfare; the discussion gives priority to helping an enemy's animal in order to subdue hostility, a reason distinct from relieving animal suffering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8cf88dd7297d78058eb", "ref": "Bava Metzia 85a", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "calf made for slaughter, then compassion", "note": "The commentary juxtaposes an animal made for work with kindness to it; the story first faults Rabbi Judah the Patriarch for denying a frightened calf mercy and later relieves him after he protects small creatures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0fe2224a7e8c9d4019a1", "ref": "Berakhot 40a", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "feed beast before eating", "note": "The commentary's right action is practical care of a mount; the discussion derives priority for feeding one's animal before oneself from the scriptural order of livestock grass and human satisfaction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfb4213cac841e5b00af", "ref": "Exodus Rabbah 2:2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Moses carries thirsty lamb", "note": "The commentary tests good conduct by how a person treats a beast; this tradition makes Moses' gentle carrying of a thirsty lamb a reason he is fit to shepherd people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-892b97751dc5d59eaf00", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 2:16", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work requires perseverance", "note": "The saying that the work is not yours to finish, yet you are not free to desist, gives labor a patience dimension close to the commentary's joining of work and ṣabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d09925dba33b9e885680", "ref": "Mishnah, Avot 3:5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoke of Torah", "note": "The Mishnah contrasts accepting Torah's yoke with bearing the yoke of worldly rule and labor; this makes a human's moral load a chosen discipline, not merely physical weight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a13c0a5a13ddca45b15", "ref": "Pirkei Avot 3:5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoke of Torah and worldly yoke", "note": "The commentary makes a human an ethical load bearer; this saying contrasts accepting the yoke of Torah with the burdens of government and livelihood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d935b78d0f744b83b1d", "ref": "Shabbat 128b", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "cushions for trapped animal", "note": "The commentary calls kindness to a strained beast good action; the rabbinic discussion permits placing cushions to help an animal fallen into a pit because of its suffering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fae425b20ac00d184fd3", "ref": "Sirach 33:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey's fodder and burden", "note": "Sirach pairs fodder, stick, and burden for a donkey with bread, discipline, and work for a servant; it directly compares animal load and human labor, while its social hierarchy differs from the commentary's shared moral image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ca4c1170db90e38add4", "ref": "Sirach 33:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burdens for donkey and work for servant", "note": "The commentary calls a camel naturally suited for work yet warns against overload; Sirach groups a donkey's fodder and burdens with a servant's bread and work, making the labor analogy explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb6026890d7b7df82287", "ref": "Sirach 38:25-26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "plowman's toil", "note": "Sirach says the farmer's wisdom is in his fieldwork and labor; this gives the commentary's working animal and human labor a specific agricultural setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd3434229ad3a63aa0ec", "ref": "Sirach 51:26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "neck placed beneath wisdom's yoke", "note": "The commentary asks which burden is fitting for a person; the teacher invites pupils to put their necks under wisdom's yoke and receive instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4509efc3e21bc9bbeb9", "ref": "Sirach 51:27", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "labor leads to abundant rest", "note": "The commentary links exertion on a hard route to its result; the teacher says he labored a little and found much rest in wisdom."}
