{"connection_id": "BC-9d935b78d0f744b83b1d", "ref": "Shabbat 128b", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "cushions for trapped animal", "note": "The commentary calls kindness to a strained beast good action; the rabbinic discussion permits placing cushions to help an animal fallen into a pit because of its suffering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fae425b20ac00d184fd3", "ref": "Sirach 33:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey's fodder and burden", "note": "Sirach pairs fodder, stick, and burden for a donkey with bread, discipline, and work for a servant; it directly compares animal load and human labor, while its social hierarchy differs from the commentary's shared moral image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ca4c1170db90e38add4", "ref": "Sirach 33:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burdens for donkey and work for servant", "note": "The commentary calls a camel naturally suited for work yet warns against overload; Sirach groups a donkey's fodder and burdens with a servant's bread and work, making the labor analogy explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb6026890d7b7df82287", "ref": "Sirach 38:25-26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "plowman's toil", "note": "Sirach says the farmer's wisdom is in his fieldwork and labor; this gives the commentary's working animal and human labor a specific agricultural setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd3434229ad3a63aa0ec", "ref": "Sirach 51:26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "neck placed beneath wisdom's yoke", "note": "The commentary asks which burden is fitting for a person; the teacher invites pupils to put their necks under wisdom's yoke and receive instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4509efc3e21bc9bbeb9", "ref": "Sirach 51:27", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "labor leads to abundant rest", "note": "The commentary links exertion on a hard route to its result; the teacher says he labored a little and found much rest in wisdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1961375bc5e7c78b1a3", "ref": "Sirach 6:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wisdom's yoke", "note": "Sirach tells the learner to put the neck under wisdom's yoke; unlike an animal overloaded by a driver, this is a chosen discipline that becomes fruitful."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1790fe15273d7571cca3", "ref": "Sirach 6:25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shoulder bent to carry wisdom", "note": "The commentary makes the human a bearer on an ethical route; Sirach urges the pupil to bend a shoulder and carry wisdom, recasting an animal's yoke as accepted instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1f7ae18e2a50de3b1d6", "ref": "Sirach 6:26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "keep wisdom's ways with strength", "note": "The commentary's human carries a moral load along a path; after urging the pupil to shoulder wisdom, Sirach says to follow her ways with all one's strength."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e60aa9cbad2222ec0ae2", "ref": "Sirach 6:30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wisdom's yoke becomes an ornament", "note": "Sirach continues its yoke image by saying wisdom's fetters become protection and her collar a glorious robe; this clarifies that its burden is chosen discipline, unlike the source's overworked beast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a7e6604bdfa1b46bcc4", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "journey too great for traveler", "note": "The commentary imagines a traveler weakened by the way; Elijah is fed because the coming journey is too much for him without provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baf6e78dd7ace0180846", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "journey to Horeb", "note": "Elijah eats the food provided and travels forty days and nights to Horeb; this is a sustained human journey to a mountain, with nourishment enabling endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c07b110b6a4cfc5ae08d", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.19.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk to God's mountain", "note": "The commentary's road climbs a mountain; strengthened by food, Elijah walks forty days to Horeb, the mountain of God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f6f6383eed6aeab76a6", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.25.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riding a donkey down mountain", "note": "The commentary sets the rider, animal, and steep path together; Abigail rides her donkey down a mountain approach to meet David's men."}
{"connection_id": "BC-913faff6b7ead1288a2a", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.25.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abigail dismounts donkey", "note": "The commentary notices the physical relation between rider and mount; Abigail quickly gets down from her donkey when she meets David, though the side is unspecified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bbc53eb29e4f84216f1", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.20.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary makes faith the traveler's reliable support; as Judah sets out into the wilderness, Jehoshaphat urges trust in the Lord so the people may be established."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a383006558bd9c88037", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.20.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary links a sound route to the result of trusting; in the same departure speech Jehoshaphat urges trust in the prophets so Judah may prosper, using Hebrew צלח."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c570e175695ff40ed470", "ref": "WLC:2Kgs.4.24", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "urgent pace on a donkey", "note": "The Shunammite orders her servant not to slow the donkey while she rushes to Elisha; the commentary warns about driving an animal beyond capacity. The text records urgency but does not say the donkey is harmed, so the contrast is limited."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b77151c1b415fb947e33", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.16.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "saddled donkeys provisioned for flight", "note": "The commentary places a rider, a load, and a route together; Ziba meets David in flight with saddled donkeys carrying food and wine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc2c2e55dd8b2b12971d", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.16.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkeys carry provisions", "note": "Ziba brings saddled donkeys loaded with food for David's household in the wilderness; the animals carry supplies that sustain exhausted travelers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2dbd2aaa230127f990a", "ref": "WLC:2Sam.16.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkeys to ride and wine for the faint", "note": "The commentary distinguishes humane travel from exhausting a mount; Ziba assigns donkeys to the royal household's riders and wine to those faint in the wilderness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d65ce1304deb7e05c21", "ref": "WLC:Amos.2.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "overloaded cart", "note": "The prophet pictures God weighed down like a cart full of sheaves to announce judgment; this is a concrete image of a load beyond ordinary capacity, though the speaker and moral force differ from the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d4d5ce669654e3ac9f8", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.24", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Aramaic אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary associates trust with surviving a dangerous route; Daniel emerges unharmed from the lions because he trusted his God, using the Aramaic cognate."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-80e2103b30b232d76c09", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.29", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Aramaic צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary ties faithful endurance to arriving well; after the lions' den Daniel prospers under successive kings, expressed with Aramaic צלח."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d054d8422add440f91a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ Aramaic אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's emûn is a reliable camel; Daniel is found faithful in his royal service. The table marks the Aramaic root as a sound candidate, and the context supplies the specific reliability sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-459a795aca41b408312e", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س ↔ Aramaic אנש; table sound correspondence", "note": "The source labels al-insān with Arabic ء ن س; Daniel's Aramaic figure is “like a son of man,” appearing after the beastly kingdoms. The table marks אנש as a sound candidate, and the scene makes human likeness distinct from beasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b944509057943a92e47", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human figure receives dominion", "note": "The next verse gives the human-like figure dominion over peoples and nations; beside the commentary's rider among working beasts, this extends the human-animal contrast into a vision of rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98aae8bd9fb68fd025f3", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.31", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God carries Israel like a parent", "note": "The wilderness journey is remembered as God carrying Israel as a man carries his son; the verse directly joins human travel, a bearer, and a carried person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1d9dc6fcd8c937e9597", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.31", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God carried Israel all the way", "note": "The commentary calls the human a bearer and traveler; Moses reverses the direction of carrying by saying God bore Israel like a father bearing his son throughout the wilderness way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b6eb681060678058a00", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary calls faith reliable bearing on a route; Moses says Israel did not trust the God who led it in the wilderness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f394844a1089dcc91a9", "ref": "WLC:Deut.1.33", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God seeks camp and shows the way", "note": "The commentary's trustworthy travel depends on a passable route; Moses says God went before Israel to find camping places and show the way by night and day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14ea65b1e65131278591", "ref": "WLC:Deut.10.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk God's ways and serve", "note": "The commentary puts good action on a traveled way; Moses asks Israel to walk in all God's ways, love him, and serve him with heart and soul."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dbfc8963f70674066bab", "ref": "WLC:Deut.22.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mismatched plow animals", "note": "The law forbids plowing with an ox and donkey yoked together. Read beside the commentary's warning about carrying beyond capacity, it raises the regulation of animal work; the verse itself gives no reason for the pairing rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-797ad8c77442a2e19ab4", "ref": "WLC:Deut.22.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallen beast under load", "note": "The source's care for the mount has a concrete legal counterpart: do not look away when a brother's donkey or ox has fallen; help raise it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac838676a66138cd4cc4", "ref": "WLC:Deut.22.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallen donkey or ox on the road", "note": "The commentary joins the road, the working animal, and its rider's treatment; this command requires raising a fallen beast encountered on the road."}
{"connection_id": "BC-44b7cb24aaf5a06a0716", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "working ox and food", "note": "The commentary presents the camel as a worker; the law forbids muzzling an ox while it threshes, joining animal labor to its right to eat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-487d5a035f8a7c57d083", "ref": "WLC:Deut.25.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unmuzzled working ox", "note": "The commentary's animal is born for work but must be treated well; the law forbids muzzling an ox while it treads grain, allowing the worker to eat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04442e1f94ffbf6e258f", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose life", "note": "The covenant sets life and death before Israel and commands them to choose life; beside the commentary's claim that outcomes depend on how one carries the load, walking has a consequential choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-285f72895e25e1b3b409", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eagle carries its young", "note": "The eagle spreads wings, takes its young, and bears them; this is a specific animal-carrier image of protection, unlike the commentary's working mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-719b81e04f04d45b4734", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eagle bears young on wings", "note": "The commentary's trustworthy carrier bears vulnerable travelers; Moses compares God's care to an eagle carrying its young on its pinions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80a03d5fbd7919080c92", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The source's emûn gloss describes a dependable camel; Deuteronomy calls God faithful and says his work and ways are just, carrying reliability from the animal image to divine character."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66cfa4aa279113f2f803", "ref": "WLC:Deut.8.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet preserved on long journey", "note": "The commentary dwells on tiring travel and dependable legs; Moses says Israel's foot did not swell during forty years in the wilderness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d5197741fe250d0c57b", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل ↔ עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's work glosses meet Hebrew עמל, toil: Ecclesiastes asks what gain a person has from all toil under the sun, tying labor to the question of loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9004391169ae137f5aa2", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary asks how labor on the journey relates to loss; Ecclesiastes asks what gain a human receives from all his toil under the sun."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74ebe6e095035192540b", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.5.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary imagines the losing traveler burdened at the end; Ecclesiastes says a person leaves as naked as at birth and cannot carry away anything gained by toil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d1730b0a98e984a6e87", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's source gloss emûn is a camel secure against tiring or stumbling; the verse calls Moses' supported hands steady (emunah) through battle until sunset. The root table offers only a sound candidate, with steadiness as the contextual bridge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffd1aed8e892a7d6cf2a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heavy hands held up", "note": "Moses' hands grow heavy, and Aaron and Hur hold them up until sunset; the source's carried load meets a scene where shared support sustains endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aeb8dc77eb11b7862a89", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's trusted mount is firm against fatigue; Aaron and Hur support Moses' hands so they remain steady until sunset, a bodily use of Hebrew אמונה."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a15766a6a8d65f0284b1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.18.18", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses and people wear out under heavy task", "note": "The commentary warns that even a willing bearer has a limit; Jethro tells Moses that he and the people will wear out because the judging burden is too heavy for one person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5493f603e9a97e817441", "ref": "WLC:Exod.18.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judges carry burden together", "note": "The commentary's overloading image calls for a changed distribution; Jethro proposes other judges handle smaller cases and share Moses' burden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0fe935e3579ef7f8cbf9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.19.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carried on eagle's wings", "note": "God describes bringing Israel out of Egypt as carrying them on eagles' wings; this makes the animal-carrier image an expression of rescue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-502dfef0a13764eebc87", "ref": "WLC:Exod.19.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carried on eagle wings to God", "note": "The commentary treats people as travelers under a burden; God says he carried Israel on eagles' wings and brought them to himself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cf7eff303ba51d3daab", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rest for working animals", "note": "The Sabbath command gives the ox and donkey rest and allows the servant to be refreshed; this adds a recurring limit on animal labor to the commentary's warning about overdriving a mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a4e71860ec21f1c7b17", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rest for ox and donkey", "note": "The commentary's valuable beast can be worked past its strength; Sabbath rest explicitly includes the ox and donkey as well as human workers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f894edc0f170d02c446", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return enemy's wandering beast", "note": "The commentary makes animal care a concrete good action; this command requires returning even an enemy's lost ox or donkey instead of abandoning it on the route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db95b265104d5577673e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey under burden", "note": "The commentary warns against driving a camel beyond its capacity; Exodus says to help an enemy's donkey when it collapses under its load. The law makes relief of the working animal a duty even toward an enemy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-283f112b78733a018589", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallen donkey under its burden", "note": "The commentary makes care for an overdriven mount part of righteous action; this law requires helping even an enemy's donkey lying under its load, giving that care a concrete ethical demand."}
{"connection_id": "BC-613f34ce792b27dd149b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "family rides donkey on journey", "note": "The commentary gives the rider's side of a carrying animal a place; Moses puts his wife and sons on a donkey for the journey back to Egypt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84c4f50c389d6614f788", "ref": "WLC:Exod.5.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary criticizes making a carrier exceed its strength; Egyptian overseers beat the workers for missing their prescribed brick quota, using Hebrew חק for a demanded measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6fa9dbfe8254f8486b9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.5.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brick quota despite lost straw", "note": "The commentary criticizes driving a worker beyond capacity; Pharaoh preserves the full brick quota while removing supplied straw, an imposed human analogue to overloading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-91f84e079f2f5fd66516", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The Arabic source label āmanū means belief; Genesis uses the Hebrew root אמנ when Abram trusts YHWH, and that trust is counted as righteousness. The root link is a candidate, and the verse specifies trust's object and result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a83cae86a25acb8e1db7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary puts faith beside good action; Abraham trusts the Lord and that trust is reckoned to him as righteousness, a distinct relation between the two."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b016faafaadcd31bc501", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey and mountain journey", "note": "Abraham saddles a donkey and prepares wood for the journey to Moriah; the animal carries the party's travel burden toward a mountain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10067f089484c2ad4e09", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey saddled for commanded journey", "note": "The commentary's human begins a consequential route with a mount; Abraham saddles his donkey and sets out to perform the command concerning Isaac."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b86f978c6d90cee32d56", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey left at foot of ascent", "note": "The commentary distinguishes mounted travel from the climb ahead; Abraham leaves the donkey with his servants before he and Isaac continue on foot to worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1cf7f2661afcb8cf3d2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Isaac carries wood uphill", "note": "Isaac carries the wood for the offering as father and son climb; beside the source's loaded camel and mountain road, the human traveler also bears a physical load."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e90dd14c06dda1b08e87", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wood borne up the mountain", "note": "The commentary places the carrier and burden on a steep route; after leaving the donkey, Abraham puts the sacrificial wood on Isaac, who carries it onward with him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a698b4460b673df52495", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels carrying goods", "note": "The servant takes ten camels and Abraham's goods on a long journey; this is a specific caravan scene joining camel, human travel, and carried property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc23e2c74bc52c200476", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "care for thirsty camels", "note": "Rebekah draws water until the camels finish drinking; beside the commentary's reliable mount and care for an animal, hospitality includes the beasts' needs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a2d766d49690fa5150e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water drawn for camels", "note": "The commentary glosses right action as kindness to a beast; Rebekah offers to draw water for the servant's camels until they finish drinking."}
