{"connection_id": "BC-91f84e079f2f5fd66516", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The Arabic source label āmanū means belief; Genesis uses the Hebrew root אמנ when Abram trusts YHWH, and that trust is counted as righteousness. The root link is a candidate, and the verse specifies trust's object and result."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a83cae86a25acb8e1db7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary puts faith beside good action; Abraham trusts the Lord and that trust is reckoned to him as righteousness, a distinct relation between the two."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b016faafaadcd31bc501", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey and mountain journey", "note": "Abraham saddles a donkey and prepares wood for the journey to Moriah; the animal carries the party's travel burden toward a mountain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10067f089484c2ad4e09", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey saddled for commanded journey", "note": "The commentary's human begins a consequential route with a mount; Abraham saddles his donkey and sets out to perform the command concerning Isaac."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b86f978c6d90cee32d56", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey left at foot of ascent", "note": "The commentary distinguishes mounted travel from the climb ahead; Abraham leaves the donkey with his servants before he and Isaac continue on foot to worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1cf7f2661afcb8cf3d2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Isaac carries wood uphill", "note": "Isaac carries the wood for the offering as father and son climb; beside the source's loaded camel and mountain road, the human traveler also bears a physical load."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e90dd14c06dda1b08e87", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wood borne up the mountain", "note": "The commentary places the carrier and burden on a steep route; after leaving the donkey, Abraham puts the sacrificial wood on Isaac, who carries it onward with him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a698b4460b673df52495", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels carrying goods", "note": "The servant takes ten camels and Abraham's goods on a long journey; this is a specific caravan scene joining camel, human travel, and carried property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc23e2c74bc52c200476", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "care for thirsty camels", "note": "Rebekah draws water until the camels finish drinking; beside the commentary's reliable mount and care for an animal, hospitality includes the beasts' needs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a2d766d49690fa5150e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water drawn for camels", "note": "The commentary glosses right action as kindness to a beast; Rebekah offers to draw water for the servant's camels until they finish drinking."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6485ec71089bb2ba39f3", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.21", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ل ح ↔ צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary glosses aslahtu as treating a beast well; beside camels on a marriage journey, the servant wonders whether YHWH has made his journey prosper. This is a cautious root-and-scene link, not a claim that both words mean the same thing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c24955d4c267634918c1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.21", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary has a well-worn route and trustworthy mount; Abraham's servant watches Rebekah at the camels' watering place to learn whether the Lord has prospered his journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-58a415bd4afea2460a87", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels unloaded and fed", "note": "The commentary sees work, burden, and humane handling together; at the house the camels are unloaded, given straw and fodder, and the travelers receive water."}

