{"connection_id": "BC-9558b3cfc8bbba10be02", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.40", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary asks whether a journey reaches its end well; Abraham's servant recalls the promise that an angel would accompany him and prosper his way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-209e711f3db21d7d6578", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.61", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rebekah rides a camel", "note": "Rebekah and her attendants mount camels and follow Abraham's servant to Isaac, a close scene of human travel on the animal the commentary describes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-41a105d1d2cdb35bfea6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.61", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "women ride camels on journey", "note": "The commentary's human mounts and travels beside a camel; Rebekah and her attendants mount camels and follow Abraham's servant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e2a0eb296cb6297af24", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.64", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rebekah dismounts camel", "note": "The commentary explicitly attends to the human side used for mounting; Rebekah gets down from her camel on seeing Isaac, though this verse does not name a side."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b79d2423e82e3cab986", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human toil ending in dust", "note": "The commentary makes the human a laboring traveler facing loss; Adam must eat by the sweat of his face until he returns to the ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69366dad42407b1fcfed", "ref": "WLC:Gen.31.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "family carried on camels", "note": "Jacob sets his children and wives on camels to flee; the verse joins the human family and camel travel, though its story is flight rather than ordinary work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29669ab288160844721b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.31.42", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل ↔ עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The source glosses ya'mele as a camel made for work and calls the animal's legs workers; Jacob names the toil of his hands after tending Laban's flocks. The table's root link joins work-beast and human labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11a3f72cfbc58a4867dd", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flocks die if driven hard", "note": "The commentary condemns driving a beast beyond its strength; Jacob warns Esau that nursing flocks and herds would all die if driven hard for even one day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d743edcd794eb9cc924", "ref": "WLC:Gen.33.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pace matched to livestock and children", "note": "The commentary's good rider respects the mount's capacity; Jacob promises to proceed slowly at the pace of the livestock and children in his care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3822f0e3379c82830e2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "caravan camels bear goods", "note": "The commentary's camel is a pack animal on a long road; the Ishmaelite caravan's camels carry trade goods down to Egypt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-109c2f9d7bbc4c847d6f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "loaded donkeys carry journey provisions", "note": "The commentary makes a beast both load bearer and support for travelers; Joseph sends loaded male and female donkeys with goods and food for his father's journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e45e24a285982be4c3a6", "ref": "WLC:Gen.49.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Issachar as strong donkey", "note": "The commentary pictures a person through a laboring mount; the blessing likens Issachar to a sturdy donkey, making the animal a figure for human capacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c82093112c50705b7c2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.49.15", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shoulder bent to bear", "note": "The commentary asks what a bearer can rightly carry; Issachar sees a desirable resting place yet bends his shoulder to a burden and becomes a laborer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d852b441c3df8620079", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن ↔ אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The source's iman is paired with deeds; Habakkuk says the righteous live by faithfulness, in a vision whose fulfillment must be awaited. The shared-root candidate links trust to endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb3118150ca2703ed7b6", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary pairs trustworthiness with enduring the journey; Habakkuk says the righteous lives by faithfulness while the proud person lacks uprightness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-758e265972708d20c6b8", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "loss of herds and crops", "note": "The prophet lists failed fruit trees and empty stalls, a concrete material loss; this gives the surah's opening claim of loss an agricultural scene before the response in the next verses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2cb078c11e867c52874f", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "livestock absent before hard ascent", "note": "The commentary joins a beast and a steep road in the endurance image; Habakkuk names the failure of flocks and herds before speaking of feet fitted for the heights."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe9da59daac31749be3c", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.18", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rejoicing amid loss", "note": "Despite the failed harvest and absent herd, the speaker rejoices in YHWH; this supplies a specific response to loss unlike a simple accounting of what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a95a1ec0b5e015f514a2", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.18", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joy despite loss of herds", "note": "The commentary's journey is evaluated by response to loss; after listing failed crops and absent animals, Habakkuk resolves to rejoice in God before describing his strengthened feet."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0eb6210c2e943622e65b", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet made sure on heights", "note": "After naming loss, the prophet says God gives feet like a deer's to tread on high places; the verse joins endurance through deprivation to the source's steep mountain path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b253d5acf5bf217b281d", "ref": "WLC:Hab.3.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feet fitted for heights", "note": "The commentary pictures legs doing the work of a steep route; Habakkuk says God makes his feet like a deer's and enables him to tread high places."}
{"connection_id": "BC-beb93040bf752adcf407", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God teaches Israel to walk", "note": "God says he taught Ephraim to walk and took them by the arms; this reverses the source's human rider and animal's working legs into a caregiver supporting a dependent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f48b6fed8ac69b55dc88", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoke lifted and fed", "note": "God leads with cords of kindness, lifts the yoke from Israel's jaws, and bends to feed them; the image directly answers the source's warning against overdriving a beast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-652de54416791a048bf7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animal knows its feeder; people do not", "note": "The commentary contrasts a trustworthy beast with a losing human; Isaiah says an ox knows its owner and a donkey its master's crib, while Israel lacks understanding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fdeb98ba8e06470f0d8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.2.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "up the mountain to learn ways", "note": "The commentary joins a mountain ascent to righteous travel; the nations invite one another up the Lord's mountain so they may learn his ways and walk in his paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ff61d44fe778846b5ad", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary makes trust part of the way out of loss; the gates open to a righteous nation keeping faithfulness, joining fidelity to the hoped-for destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9cc0f07408617c3de443", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels carrying treasure", "note": "The oracle pictures Judah's envoys carrying wealth on young donkeys and camels through a dangerous land. It joins loaded beasts to a journey whose destination is trouble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c71345828964ca929a9a", "ref": "WLC:Isa.30.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkeys and camels bearing treasure", "note": "The commentary's pack animal carries loads through a difficult way; the oracle sends treasure on young donkeys and camels across a dangerous land, toward an unprofitable ally."}
{"connection_id": "BC-330f4c5a8a9f7242a964", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weak hands and knees strengthened", "note": "The commentary makes legs and endurance part of the steep way; the prophet tells the community to strengthen weak hands and make feeble knees firm before the promised return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bc1f035c8535bc55ad4", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "highway of holiness", "note": "The prophet describes a raised highway on which the redeemed walk; this turns the source's opened, well-traveled road into a route marked by holiness and return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ff8003196116e18a0fa", "ref": "WLC:Isa.35.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "highway of holiness", "note": "The commentary's opened road carries a moral consequence; Isaiah describes a highway defined by holiness, with access distinguished by the traveler's conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a21462137356b76204c6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shepherd carries lambs", "note": "The shepherd gathers lambs and carries them in his bosom while gently leading nursing ewes; the source's animal-care gloss meets an image of carrying that protects the vulnerable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54646901f882a6029eae", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carry lambs, gently lead mothers", "note": "The commentary glosses right action as care for a beast; the divine shepherd carries lambs close and gently leads those with young."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7befdbe8c48bab8b080", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.29", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strength given to the faint", "note": "The commentary's traveler and reliable animal must resist exhaustion; God gives power to the faint and increases strength for those without it before the running and walking of Isaiah 40:31."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b08a0177bf4503242a3e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prepare highway in desert", "note": "The commentary's open road is a condition of journeying; the prophetic voice calls for a highway to be prepared for God in the wilderness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-273ce7f57af19f0452b7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.31", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "renewed strength for the journey", "note": "Those who wait for YHWH renew their strength, mount up with wings, run without weariness, and walk without fainting; this directly joins trust, animal-like movement, and endurance on the road."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e38bb85153041512931d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.31", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "run without weariness, walk without fainting", "note": "The commentary praises a dependable mount that does not tire or stumble; those waiting for the Lord receive renewed strength to run and walk without exhaustion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f71be3901d7c6898c2e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountains lowered for a road", "note": "Isaiah imagines valleys lifted and mountains made low so a highway is level; beside the commentary's steep mountain path, the prophetic route is made passable by divine action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94002d0200ab958d69d5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain lowered for way", "note": "The commentary's route climbs a hard mountain; this vision has mountains and hills brought low to make a level approach."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48de172bc1cd27261c5a", "ref": "WLC:Isa.43.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "new road in wilderness", "note": "The commentary calls the road opened by much travel; God instead promises to make a new way in the wilderness, supplying a different source of passability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-364ab1c047d168be6e78", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols carried by beasts", "note": "Unlike the commentary's mount carrying a traveler, Bel and Nebo are cargo on beasts' backs; the idols themselves stoop as their carriers tire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6dbe2cbd44d43cd2405", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols loaded on weary beasts", "note": "The commentary asks whether loads are borne rightly by animals; the oracle depicts idols as cargo on tired beasts, a pointed image of a burdensome false trust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5f0b8d149e35a79f3c9", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "powerless cargo", "note": "The idols cannot rescue their load or themselves from captivity; Isaiah reverses the image of a dependable carrier by making the supposed gods helpless burdens."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8ac6142e9172274cbe6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols cannot rescue their burden", "note": "The commentary links correct bearing with loss or rescue at journey's end; these gods cannot deliver the load and themselves go into captivity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb662144a3a03ffa6612", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God carries Israel", "note": "The commentary says the human took up the trust; Isaiah's God says Israel has been carried from the womb, shifting the bearer from human to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa39bd2b7730f395c143", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "God bears the people", "note": "The commentary's human shoulders an entrusted load; this oracle reverses the burden image by calling Israel a people carried by God from the womb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b91ae511d3ff3ad536e1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carrying into old age", "note": "God promises to carry Israel through old age and gray hairs; this gives the load image a sustaining, lifelong meaning rather than an exhausting one."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5858d263cebce685cf5a", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "I will carry and save", "note": "The commentary worries about a carrier weakening under load; God promises to carry his people even into old age and to save them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dbe273c0752b30087916", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shouldered idol cannot move or answer", "note": "The commentary asks what a carrier's load can do for him; worshippers bear an idol on the shoulder and set it down, but it cannot move or answer their cry."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47d690b7d57d55d9ba92", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل ↔ עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's source labels from ع م ل describe work and labor; Isaiah says the servant sees the toil of his soul and is satisfied, then bears others' iniquities. This joins labor, burden, and its outcome through the table's candidate root."}

