{"connection_id": "BC-0aebcdd14dfe5c4a3a6b", "ref": "WLC:Judg.5.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riders and walkers on a road", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a rider from travelers called walkers; Deborah addresses those riding donkeys and those walking the road in one summons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a860dd14977c1725d779", "ref": "WLC:Lev.16.21", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquities placed on goat", "note": "The commentary's losers carry weights on their backs; Aaron places Israel's confessed sins on the live goat's head, turning guilt into a visible animal load."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb7d251581eb20e68807", "ref": "WLC:Lev.16.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "goat bears sins into wilderness", "note": "The commentary's load and wilderness journey meet in the goat that bears the people's iniquities away to an uninhabited land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-580d7564b6eebf664af5", "ref": "WLC:Mic.4.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain ascent to learn walking paths", "note": "The commentary joins a steep destination to the way of right action; Micah's parallel oracle has nations ascend God's mountain to learn his ways and walk his paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b59eb682add2eae8e1d", "ref": "WLC:Mic.6.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "justice, kindness, humble walking", "note": "The commentary makes goodness more than the traveler's destination; Micah joins doing justice and loving kindness to walking humbly with God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c97515a651a3de3a7b69", "ref": "WLC:Neh.2.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mount cannot pass", "note": "The wall's ruin is so obstructive that Nehemiah's animal cannot pass beneath him; this gives the road-and-mount image a concrete blocked route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ff9cbdacb8c3172ea8d", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses asked to carry people like infant", "note": "The commentary portrays the human as a load bearer; Moses asks whether he conceived the people, since God has told him to carry them like a nurse carrying a child to the promised land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0174de2ff34b66c90027", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burden too heavy to carry alone", "note": "The commentary describes a load beyond the carrier's strength; Moses says he cannot carry the people alone because the burden is too heavy for him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc0de5a7532e8ddb170f", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders share the human burden", "note": "The commentary warns against a load exceeding its bearer; God promises that the elders will bear the burden of the people with Moses so he will not carry it alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff7c66c6a4b0fe975222", "ref": "WLC:Num.12.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses faithful in God's house", "note": "The source glosses emûn as a dependable camel; God calls Moses faithful in the divine household. The Hebrew אמנ root is the table's sound-correspondence candidate, and this verse gives the human stewardship sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc9f50f04a3e21cc16c0", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey beaten for leaving the road", "note": "Balaam strikes his donkey when it turns off the road to avoid the angel; the animal that seems to stumble is responding to danger its rider cannot see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7483118afeb9b7578fc5", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey leaves road on seeing danger", "note": "The commentary's dependable mount can stumble or fail on a road; Balaam's donkey turns from the path because it sees the angel that its rider cannot see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c36c2fb1f40592ab0cc", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey presses rider's foot", "note": "The commentary singles out animal legs and the rider's relation to the beast; Balaam's donkey presses his foot against a wall while avoiding the angel, and he strikes it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1ef6af32629e88a9079", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.27", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey lies down under rider", "note": "The commentary's overdriven animal cannot sustain movement; Balaam's donkey lies down beneath him and receives another beating."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a962c5481bced7605159", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey speaks under mistreatment", "note": "The source distinguishes the human rider from the animal he rides; Balaam's donkey speaks to protest repeated blows, giving the working animal a voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-495b3c23a285217f12a7", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "animal protests repeated beating", "note": "The commentary imagines a rider either caring for or overdriving a mount; Balaam's donkey speaks to challenge his third beating, exposing the rider's failure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9571a5bb1c4f687c39f1", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "driver's crooked way", "note": "The angel asks why Balaam beat his donkey and says Balaam's way is perverse; the rider's treatment, rather than the animal's refusal, is judged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af44f78b3a49f078ef11", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.33", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey saves its rider", "note": "The donkey's turns preserve Balaam's life, and the angel says it would otherwise have killed him. This reverses the assumption that the mount is merely a burden-bearing tool."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be997ba780100b5a808d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous care for animals", "note": "The commentary glosses aslahtu as treating the animal well; Proverbs says a righteous person knows the needs of a beast, while the wicked person's compassion is cruel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-457e294f7c8cb1dcf05e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous person and animal's life", "note": "The commentary glosses doing good as treating a beast well; the proverb makes the righteous person's concern for an animal's needs a test of righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4035237eb7a7f839900", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ox strength and harvest", "note": "The proverb says an empty manger is clean without oxen, but abundant crops come through the ox's strength; it names the cost and yield of animal labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd68172b1b0e88db3352", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "know the state of the flock", "note": "The proverb tells the keeper to know the condition of his flock and attend to the herds; this makes the commentary's dependable animal and good treatment an ongoing duty of attention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21a0334807ec0dea9490", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk securely without stumbling", "note": "The commentary praises a mount that will not stumble; the wisdom traveler is promised a secure way and an unslipping foot."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c1cc91407d1a46acf0a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust beyond one's own understanding", "note": "The proverb commands trust in YHWH rather than reliance on one's own understanding; this makes the commentary's iman-as-reliability image a relation of dependence, not merely confidence in a mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02a2285dd660e067550b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust makes the path straight", "note": "The proverb says to acknowledge God in all one's ways and he will make the paths straight; it joins trust to the road image developed by the commentary."}

