{"connection_id": "BC-fda6062d2c102b43022b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "toil and bearing iniquities", "note": "The commentary joins human labor, right action, and burdens; the servant sees the fruit of his toil and bears others' iniquities, changing who bears the load."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a85690c2a89fe6b8848d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "suffering carried by another", "note": "The servant bears others' sicknesses and carries their pains; this gives the commentary's burden image a different bearer, one who takes others' suffering upon himself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cdfaafc21e28c7c5914", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "griefs borne, sorrows carried", "note": "The commentary turns loss into a weight borne on the journey; the servant bears others' griefs and carries their sorrows, specifying a load distinct from the iniquities in Isaiah 53:11."}
{"connection_id": "BC-866a498f9765ee4def9f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guidance through dry places", "note": "The commentary's traveler must keep moving through hard terrain; Isaiah promises continual guidance and renewed strength even in parched places after acts of justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0cb882e3335400efa702", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "undo the straps of the yoke", "note": "The commentary's good action refuses a load that exhausts another creature; Isaiah calls the chosen fast one that unties oppressive yokes and frees the burdened."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e90b70a21e8ac900bae6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remove the yoke and harmful speech", "note": "The commentary places good action beside truth on the road; Isaiah promises a divine answer if the people remove the yoke and stop malicious gesture and speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ed17612fe158efada61", "ref": "WLC:Isa.63.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carried the redeemed through former days", "note": "The commentary's human load and journey are reversed when God lifts and carries the people in compassion through their days."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a83da4bde823115c19c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "camels in the return journey", "note": "The nations bring Israel's exiles back to Jerusalem on camels and other mounts; the verse turns caravan travel into a restoration procession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2abf66ed0088ec3c79e", "ref": "WLC:Jer.12.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary makes proper conduct matter to the traveler's outcome; Jeremiah asks why the wicked person's way prospers, a pointed complication using Hebrew צלח."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18bec94a3e2621dd1512", "ref": "WLC:Jer.12.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "footmen and horses", "note": "God challenges Jeremiah: if footmen have wearied him, how will he contend with horses? The image tests a traveler's endurance as conditions become harder, matching the commentary's concern with capacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6b3b2c8da88dc1813b1", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight path without stumbling", "note": "The commentary praises a mount that does not stumble and an opened path; God leads the returning people beside water on a straight road where they will not stumble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2767129c3e09f1d82a2", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good ancient path and rest", "note": "The commentary's beaten way becomes a moral journey; Jeremiah calls people to ask for the good way, walk in it, and find rest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd2cc81e9219d9f0fc3a", "ref": "WLC:Job.28.26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary joins rightful measure and a path; Job says God prescribed a measure for rain and a way for thunder, using Hebrew חק for prescription."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a07e38b5b3e7b9034a1", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "yoking the wild ox", "note": "The poem asks whether a human can bind the wild ox to a furrow; this tests whether strength and labor can simply be imposed on an animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-229176212f6e0966e0f1", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trusting the animal's strength", "note": "God asks whether Job would rely on the wild ox's great strength; the question specifically joins trust to an animal's carrying and working capacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27bfaf4f41cb2f079d90", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animal expected to gather grain", "note": "The poem asks whether the wild ox will bring the harvest into the barn; it contrasts a trained worker such as the source's ya'mele with a strong animal that cannot be assumed to serve human labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-350189a893c80e4ba313", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.19", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "horse's strength", "note": "God asks who gives the horse its might; beside the commentary's emaciated horse driven too hard, Job locates strength in the creature's given power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b70d38a276af07c558b", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wild donkey released", "note": "The poem asks who released the wild donkey, which scorns the city and its driver; beside the source's trained work camel, it presents an animal outside human control."}
{"connection_id": "BC-edd8d2d84284eb5c1179", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain range as pasture", "note": "The wild donkey searches the mountains for pasture rather than following a human road; this gives the source's animal-and-mountain pairing a different, free animal's route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f1360d85f5292333291", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wild ox refuses service", "note": "God asks whether the wild ox will serve or stay by a manger; beside the commentary's camel trained for work, Job distinguishes a creature's nature from its use as a work animal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d83cb488fa2e37376edf", "ref": "WLC:Job.4.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء ن س / Hebrew אנש; table sound correspondence, BDB notes Arabic cognate", "note": "The commentary places good action against human loss; Eliphaz asks whether mortal enosh can be righteous before God, exposing the traveler's frailty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71dbf5d8cd005d3d5e42", "ref": "WLC:Job.5.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary spreads work across beast, limbs, road, and walker; Job says a human is born to toil, bringing that labor back onto the human bearer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-899f854ef2dc00546f41", "ref": "WLC:Job.7.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human under divine scrutiny", "note": "Job asks what a mortal is that God magnifies and tests him; this is a specific counterpoint to the commentary's human traveler whose fate depends on what he bears."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3fdcb2448e438fbb6eaa", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ص ل ح ↔ צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The source's ṣalaḥ gloss is treating the animal well; Joshua uses the corresponding Hebrew root for making one's way prosper through keeping the Torah. The shared root and path are specific, though the source's care sense differs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af85a566b7e8272c21ce", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary joins right action to the route's outcome; Joshua is told to perform the written instruction so his way will prosper, using Hebrew צלח for that success."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0aebcdd14dfe5c4a3a6b", "ref": "WLC:Judg.5.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riders and walkers on a road", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a rider from travelers called walkers; Deborah addresses those riding donkeys and those walking the road in one summons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a860dd14977c1725d779", "ref": "WLC:Lev.16.21", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquities placed on goat", "note": "The commentary's losers carry weights on their backs; Aaron places Israel's confessed sins on the live goat's head, turning guilt into a visible animal load."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb7d251581eb20e68807", "ref": "WLC:Lev.16.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "goat bears sins into wilderness", "note": "The commentary's load and wilderness journey meet in the goat that bears the people's iniquities away to an uninhabited land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-580d7564b6eebf664af5", "ref": "WLC:Mic.4.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain ascent to learn walking paths", "note": "The commentary joins a steep destination to the way of right action; Micah's parallel oracle has nations ascend God's mountain to learn his ways and walk his paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b59eb682add2eae8e1d", "ref": "WLC:Mic.6.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "justice, kindness, humble walking", "note": "The commentary makes goodness more than the traveler's destination; Micah joins doing justice and loving kindness to walking humbly with God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c97515a651a3de3a7b69", "ref": "WLC:Neh.2.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mount cannot pass", "note": "The wall's ruin is so obstructive that Nehemiah's animal cannot pass beneath him; this gives the road-and-mount image a concrete blocked route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ff9cbdacb8c3172ea8d", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses asked to carry people like infant", "note": "The commentary portrays the human as a load bearer; Moses asks whether he conceived the people, since God has told him to carry them like a nurse carrying a child to the promised land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0174de2ff34b66c90027", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.14", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burden too heavy to carry alone", "note": "The commentary describes a load beyond the carrier's strength; Moses says he cannot carry the people alone because the burden is too heavy for him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc0de5a7532e8ddb170f", "ref": "WLC:Num.11.17", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "elders share the human burden", "note": "The commentary warns against a load exceeding its bearer; God promises that the elders will bear the burden of the people with Moses so he will not carry it alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff7c66c6a4b0fe975222", "ref": "WLC:Num.12.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses faithful in God's house", "note": "The source glosses emûn as a dependable camel; God calls Moses faithful in the divine household. The Hebrew אמנ root is the table's sound-correspondence candidate, and this verse gives the human stewardship sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc9f50f04a3e21cc16c0", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey beaten for leaving the road", "note": "Balaam strikes his donkey when it turns off the road to avoid the angel; the animal that seems to stumble is responding to danger its rider cannot see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7483118afeb9b7578fc5", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey leaves road on seeing danger", "note": "The commentary's dependable mount can stumble or fail on a road; Balaam's donkey turns from the path because it sees the angel that its rider cannot see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c36c2fb1f40592ab0cc", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey presses rider's foot", "note": "The commentary singles out animal legs and the rider's relation to the beast; Balaam's donkey presses his foot against a wall while avoiding the angel, and he strikes it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1ef6af32629e88a9079", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.27", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey lies down under rider", "note": "The commentary's overdriven animal cannot sustain movement; Balaam's donkey lies down beneath him and receives another beating."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a962c5481bced7605159", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey speaks under mistreatment", "note": "The source distinguishes the human rider from the animal he rides; Balaam's donkey speaks to protest repeated blows, giving the working animal a voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-495b3c23a285217f12a7", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.28", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "animal protests repeated beating", "note": "The commentary imagines a rider either caring for or overdriving a mount; Balaam's donkey speaks to challenge his third beating, exposing the rider's failure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9571a5bb1c4f687c39f1", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "driver's crooked way", "note": "The angel asks why Balaam beat his donkey and says Balaam's way is perverse; the rider's treatment, rather than the animal's refusal, is judged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af44f78b3a49f078ef11", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.33", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey saves its rider", "note": "The donkey's turns preserve Balaam's life, and the angel says it would otherwise have killed him. This reverses the assumption that the mount is merely a burden-bearing tool."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be997ba780100b5a808d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous care for animals", "note": "The commentary glosses aslahtu as treating the animal well; Proverbs says a righteous person knows the needs of a beast, while the wicked person's compassion is cruel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-457e294f7c8cb1dcf05e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous person and animal's life", "note": "The commentary glosses doing good as treating a beast well; the proverb makes the righteous person's concern for an animal's needs a test of righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4035237eb7a7f839900", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ox strength and harvest", "note": "The proverb says an empty manger is clean without oxen, but abundant crops come through the ox's strength; it names the cost and yield of animal labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd68172b1b0e88db3352", "ref": "WLC:Prov.27.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "know the state of the flock", "note": "The proverb tells the keeper to know the condition of his flock and attend to the herds; this makes the commentary's dependable animal and good treatment an ongoing duty of attention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21a0334807ec0dea9490", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk securely without stumbling", "note": "The commentary praises a mount that will not stumble; the wisdom traveler is promised a secure way and an unslipping foot."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c1cc91407d1a46acf0a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust beyond one's own understanding", "note": "The proverb commands trust in YHWH rather than reliance on one's own understanding; this makes the commentary's iman-as-reliability image a relation of dependence, not merely confidence in a mount."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02a2285dd660e067550b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust makes the path straight", "note": "The proverb says to acknowledge God in all one's ways and he will make the paths straight; it joins trust to the road image developed by the commentary."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-f9a0e89957f12440ca4d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.30.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary asks what a creature may rightly bear; the prayer requests an apportioned amount of bread, using Hebrew חק for the fitting measure rather than Arabic truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54b8108d8f472bf72817", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.12", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking and running without stumbling", "note": "The commentary's dependable carrier does not trip despite continued travel; wisdom promises that a walker's steps will not be cramped and that a runner will not stumble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c095cb1d5cb9536895b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.26", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "consider the path of your feet", "note": "The proverb tells the hearer to make the path of the feet level and keep the way steady; it connects the commentary's road to deliberate, careful conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e80956c736e1c460e36", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's good action and open road imply a successful arrival; the righteous tree bears fruit and prospers in all it does, using Hebrew צלח for success."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9473eb6a2531365fc135", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways and their ends", "note": "The psalm says YHWH knows the way of the righteous while the way of the wicked perishes; this makes the commentary's road and final loss an explicit ethical route with an outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d63579bb97de46f2c4e9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of wicked perishes", "note": "The commentary's human road ends in gain or loss; the psalm sets the known way of the righteous against the way of the wicked that perishes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13658e5fb1d688bd4297", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.21", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lions seek food at night", "note": "The psalm says young lions roar for prey and seek their food from God; the animal has its own work and dependence within the created order, alongside the human labor named in verse 23."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6406ee99eed592f287b9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.22", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animals withdraw at sunrise", "note": "At sunrise the lions withdraw to their dens; the next verse sends humans out to work. This day-night ordering gives the commentary's human and animal labor a distinct shared setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cecce30d3d8473c969d5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human work through the day", "note": "The psalm says a human goes out to work until evening, after describing animals' night activity; this places human toil and animal life in one daily rhythm."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab2d2c5a43d455c884eb", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wanderers find no road to city", "note": "The commentary makes the human a traveler whose outcome matters; wanderers in the wilderness cannot find a way to an inhabited city."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0b08c4f4442d67c8a7c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "travelers hungry, thirsty and faint", "note": "The commentary's rider or walker can be exhausted by the route; the same wanderers grow hungry and thirsty until their lives faint within them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-759a01d522526a8a6e46", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight road reaches inhabited city", "note": "The commentary's beaten road points toward an end; God leads the lost wanderers by a straight way until they reach an inhabited city."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c9c7dc170095091ebab", "ref": "WLC:Ps.118.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's road needs a favorable end; the pilgrimage psalm prays for success with Hebrew צלח as worshippers approach the sanctuary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dad207db8f810df48d5b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary links confidence with an opened moral road; the psalmist says he has chosen the way of faithfulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17276c67b30eeed7cbbf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "run the way of commandments", "note": "The commentary turns a repeatedly trodden path into moral action; the psalmist proposes to run the way of God's commandments when his heart is enlarged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a088b73662ef6f11d2f2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.33", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's haqq becomes what rightly governs a difficult route; the psalm asks to be taught the way of God's statutes, using the Hebrew root for what is prescribed rather than Arabic truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-020f94ffbf8b93b3461e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.54", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary brings prescribed truth onto a traveled road; the psalmist calls God's statutes songs in the house of his sojourning, combining law with temporary residence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4bf5673853c8006c2e2c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.121.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foot kept from slipping", "note": "The pilgrim song says God will not let the traveler's foot slip; beside the commentary's stony mountain road, it makes secure footing a divine protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2a42d82b015cdcfb394", "ref": "WLC:Ps.121.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foot kept from slipping", "note": "The commentary's reliable camel does not stumble on its route; the pilgrim's keeper will not let his foot slip."}
