{"connection_id": "BC-d0b08c4f4442d67c8a7c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "travelers hungry, thirsty and faint", "note": "The commentary's rider or walker can be exhausted by the route; the same wanderers grow hungry and thirsty until their lives faint within them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-759a01d522526a8a6e46", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight road reaches inhabited city", "note": "The commentary's beaten road points toward an end; God leads the lost wanderers by a straight way until they reach an inhabited city."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c9c7dc170095091ebab", "ref": "WLC:Ps.118.25", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ص ل ح / Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's road needs a favorable end; the pilgrimage psalm prays for success with Hebrew צלח as worshippers approach the sanctuary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dad207db8f810df48d5b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.30", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary links confidence with an opened moral road; the psalmist says he has chosen the way of faithfulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17276c67b30eeed7cbbf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.32", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "run the way of commandments", "note": "The commentary turns a repeatedly trodden path into moral action; the psalmist proposes to run the way of God's commandments when his heart is enlarged."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a088b73662ef6f11d2f2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.33", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's haqq becomes what rightly governs a difficult route; the psalm asks to be taught the way of God's statutes, using the Hebrew root for what is prescribed rather than Arabic truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-020f94ffbf8b93b3461e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.54", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ح ق ق / Hebrew חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary brings prescribed truth onto a traveled road; the psalmist calls God's statutes songs in the house of his sojourning, combining law with temporary residence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4bf5673853c8006c2e2c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.121.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foot kept from slipping", "note": "The pilgrim song says God will not let the traveler's foot slip; beside the commentary's stony mountain road, it makes secure footing a divine protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e2a42d82b015cdcfb394", "ref": "WLC:Ps.121.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foot kept from slipping", "note": "The commentary's reliable camel does not stumble on its route; the pilgrim's keeper will not let his foot slip."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c558da1009951b7ebe00", "ref": "WLC:Ps.127.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary implies that effort must be rightly directed; the psalm says builders toil in vain unless the Lord builds the house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97f14bca87d056fff625", "ref": "WLC:Ps.147.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "horse strength and human legs", "note": "The psalm pairs the strength of a horse with the legs of a man, the very human-animal labor relation developed by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2fea7cf8530b6bf11830", "ref": "WLC:Ps.147.11", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust rather than strength", "note": "The next verse says God's delight is in those who fear and hope in him, not in horse strength or human legs; it redirects the source's image of a reliable mount toward trust in God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bb36104a6e447c24b9a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.15.1", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "who may dwell on holy hill", "note": "The commentary's mountain is a destination that tests the traveler; this verse asks who may dwell on God's holy hill, giving the destination for the ethical walker of Psalm 15:2."}
{"connection_id": "BC-028996cdd751c1dafcf4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.15.2", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk blameless, do right, speak truth", "note": "The commentary joins action and truth to the journey; the person permitted on God's holy hill walks blamelessly, does what is right, and speaks truth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e3e3405fd99a80d661b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.18.34", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deer feet on heights", "note": "The commentary's ṣabīr is glossed as a mountain; the psalm says God gives the speaker deer-like feet and sets him securely on heights, joining sure footing, animal movement, and ascent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-375a2dbe82f913d8d606", "ref": "WLC:Ps.20.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "trust in horses", "note": "The psalm contrasts those who rely on chariots and horses with those who invoke YHWH; it questions making the animal mount the object of confidence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3827ccd3e1e011f54193", "ref": "WLC:Ps.23.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "led in right paths", "note": "The commentary connects right treatment to a traversable way; the shepherd restores and leads the speaker in paths of righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae32606e4a9890c892fe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.24.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ascent of the holy mountain", "note": "The commentary turns patience into a mountain on the route; the psalm asks who can ascend the Lord's hill and stand there."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3cfc97fc8bd7ce7e5bf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.24.4", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "clean hands for ascent", "note": "The commentary ties the difficult ascent to good action and truth; the next verse admits the person with clean hands, pure heart, and no false oath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39b3262e7d66b42be9ee", "ref": "WLC:Ps.32.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "animal controlled by bit", "note": "The psalm warns the hearer not to be like a horse or mule needing bit and bridle; beside the commentary's attentive human-animal relation, it makes control of the mount a moral metaphor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-313925c81b67c55c18c3", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trust and doing good", "note": "The psalm directly joins trusting YHWH with doing good and dwelling in the land; this is a close ethical formula for the commentary's pairing of belief and righteous deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd97823d119d4b610bae", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء م ن / Hebrew אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary places trust beside good treatment and action; this psalm joins trusting the Lord, doing good, and faithfulness in a single instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a2fe29d08d0e8555c44", "ref": "WLC:Ps.38.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquity as unbearable weight", "note": "The speaker says his iniquities are a burden too heavy for him; this makes the source's load-bearing image explicitly moral and beyond-capacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd4cd33d8bb56b58d3ba", "ref": "WLC:Ps.38.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquity as unbearable load", "note": "The commentary links loss to burdens borne at the journey's end; the psalmist's iniquities rise over his head like a weight too heavy for him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc60ac7406f39fd745f2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.55.23", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cast one's burden on God", "note": "The commentary's human carrier may be crushed by load; the psalm tells the righteous to cast that burden on the Lord, who will sustain him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d2ade618c5fa99690e15", "ref": "WLC:Ps.62.13", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deeds repaid", "note": "The psalm says God repays each person according to their work; this gives the commentary's claim that outcomes depend on how the load is carried a specific scriptural formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-321c5fc539120d0bc121", "ref": "WLC:Ps.68.20", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God bears daily", "note": "The psalm says God bears his people day by day; the commentary's human-borne trust is reframed as a load God carries for humans."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fb45b7dfaeea103f8c1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء ن س ↔ אנש; table notes BDB Arabic cognate", "note": "The commentary labels al-insān with Arabic root ء ن س; WLC's enosh belongs to אנש, whose table entry says BDB cites an Arabic cognate. The psalm asks why mortal humanity receives God's care; the next verse's animal dominion is noted separately."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d1c6cd40fd8aadf2fa7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.5", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء ن س / Hebrew אנש; table sound correspondence, BDB notes Arabic cognate", "note": "The commentary names the human alongside a mount; the psalm asks why God remembers frail enosh, framing human dignity as a question."}
{"connection_id": "BC-22b2c2c54698de1dec17", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human over animals", "note": "The psalm gives humanity rule over sheep, oxen, and other creatures; beside the commentary's human mounted on a working animal, it frames the relation as human stewardship and dominion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04ba11d3c933bc54f597", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human rule over works under his feet", "note": "The commentary's rider governs a beast's labor; the psalm grants humanity rule over the works of God's hands, a responsibility framing that relation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-614dc3181bbc0f5ca5f7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.8.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sheep and cattle under human rule", "note": "The commentary places a human beside a working animal; the psalm names flocks, cattle, and field beasts as animals under humanity's appointed dominion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5dd8f1af8b5ea50d064", "ref": "WLC:Ps.84.6", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pilgrim's strength and heart's highways", "note": "The commentary joins inner trust to a traversable way; this blessing gives strength to the pilgrim whose heart holds the highways to Zion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4bfb716212e15758db9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.84.7", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hard valley becomes a place of springs", "note": "The commentary's difficult route must be endured rather than merely named; pilgrims crossing the valley of Baca make it a place of springs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-492f9172f3d274e709d1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.84.8", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pilgrims go from strength to strength", "note": "The commentary's traveler needs endurance up to the destination; the psalm has pilgrims advancing from strength to strength toward Zion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79d6063314d76837813e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ع م ل / Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary casts mortal life as a tiring road; the psalm calls even its longer span toil and trouble before it quickly passes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12ebac20527eeee2d698", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mortal returned to dust", "note": "The commentary calls attention to the human as traveler and bearer; the psalm says God returns mortal humanity to dust, giving that journey a finite endpoint and a mortality sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50dd6d3c4d85bfcb59b3", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.3", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ء ن س / Hebrew אנש; table sound correspondence, BDB notes Arabic cognate", "note": "The commentary's human traveler faces loss; the psalm says God returns enosh to dust, stressing the mortality of the one who bears the journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce191344dfa3e6d6a120", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.10", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "warhorse removed after donkey king", "note": "The commentary considers the rider's character through his mount; following the humble king's donkey, the prophecy removes warhorses and announces peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14d3d0a33a8ea2c1a97f", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.9", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humble king on donkey", "note": "The commentary joins human character to treatment and use of a mount; Zechariah's arriving king is righteous and humble while riding a donkey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd16a4e0bfe2cff040fc", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 9:15", "target": "sec7", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body weighs on the soul", "note": "Wisdom says the perishable body weighs down the soul; this gives the source's human burden metaphor an inward, bodily meaning."}

