text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Add File: enrichment/bible/work/s103/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.s103-20261009/sec7/root_verdicts.jsonl
+{"root":"ء م ن","decision":"used","hebrew":["אמנ"],"reason":"Hebrew אמנ ranges from support and bodily steadiness to trust and fidelity; Exod.17.12 gives the page's reliable carrier image a precise firmness counterpart. The Aramaic occurrences concern trust or reliability, not camel endurance.","paragraphs":[22],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
+{"root":"ء ن س","decision":"used","hebrew":["אנש"],"reason":"BDB cites an Arabic cognate for אנש; Ps.8.5 uses enosh for mortal humanity beside animal dominion in 8.7-8. Hebrew and Aramaic אנס concern compulsion or oppression and do not illuminate the human noun here.","paragraphs":[22],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
+{"root":"ح ق ق","decision":"no_qualifying_parallel","hebrew":["חקק"],"reason":"The Hebrew forms in Exod.5.14, Job.28.26, Ps.119.33 and Prov.30.8 mean prescribed quota, statute or allotment. They do not express the camel's readiness to bear, excessive driving, or the surah's truth motif specifically enough for an additional note.","paragraphs":[22,23],"annotations":[]}
+{"root":"ص ب ر","decision":"false_friend","hebrew":["צבר"],"reason":"Hebrew צבר means heap up or a heap across its eight WLC occurrences; none denotes patience, endurance, a rocky road or a mountain as the Arabic branch does here.","paragraphs":[22,23],"annotations":[]}
+{"root":"ص ل ح","decision":"used","hebrew":["צלח"],"reason":"Hebrew and Biblical Aramaic צלח predominantly mean advance or prosper, with other homographs for vessels. Gen.24.21 places success of a journey beside camel care, but the verb itself does not mean treating an animal well; the annotation keeps that limit explicit.","paragraphs":[22],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
+{"root":"ع م ل","decision":"used","hebrew":["עמל"],"reason":"Hebrew עמל centers on wearying toil or trouble, especially in Ecclesiastes; Eccl.1.3 and 5.14 tie that toil to gain and what a person can carry away. It is narrower and darker than the Arabic page's broad work and road imagery.","paragraphs":[23],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-004"]}
*** End Patch"));

