+{"connection_id":"BC-4cdfaafc21e28c7c5914","ref":"WLC:Isa.53.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Griefs and sorrows are indeed borne by the servant, but this vicarious suffering is a separate setting from the camel-road analogy.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.53.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-866a498f9765ee4def9f","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Continual guidance and strength in dry places follow social justice in Isaiah 58; the verse contains no mount or carried load.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0cb882e3335400efa702","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Undoing oppressive yokes is human liberation in a fasting oracle, not animal overloading; Exodus 23:5 directly names a laden beast.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e90b70a21e8ac900bae6","ref":"WLC:Isa.58.9","status":"rejected","reason":"Removing the yoke and harmful speech joins ethics, but the yoke is oppression within the community, not the page's camel load.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.58.9"],"annotations":[]}
